@h1 Write a custom renderer h1@

Walk the expanded Wit AST and emit @strong your own strong@ target format — LaTeX,
plain text, RTF, a Slack payload, a JSON intermediate. The two shipped renderers
(HTML and Markdown) are working references for exactly this. For developers.

@h2 Step 1 — Get an expanded document h2@

Run @code parse code@, @code resolve code@, @code expand code@ (add
@code loadExternalData code@ first if the document loads data), then walk the
children of the expanded document.

@h2 Step 2 — Recurse on @code node.kind code@ h2@

This walk produced correct plain-text output when verified:

@@@pre
import { parse } from '@witlang/parser';
import { resolve, expand, isCoreVocabName, RESERVED_OPAQUE } from '@witlang/runtime';

const expanded = expand(resolve(parse(src, 'doc.wit')));
const param = (use, n) => use.params.find(p => p.name === n)?.value;

function renderInline(node) {
  switch (node.kind) {
    case 'text':    return node.value;
    case 'italic':  return '_'  + node.children.map(renderInline).join('') + '_';
    case 'bold':    return '**' + node.children.map(renderInline).join('') + '**';
    case 'nodeUse': return renderUse(node);
    default:        return '';
  }
}

function renderUse(use) {
  if (use.name === RESERVED_OPAQUE) return `[node type=${param(use, 'type')}]`;
  const body = (use.body ?? []).map(renderInline).join('');
  if (use.name === 'h1') return `\n# ${body}\n`;
  if (use.name === 'li') return `  - ${body}`;
  if (isCoreVocabName(use.name)) return body;  // generic core-vocab branch
  return body;                                 // user def already inlined by expand
}
pre@@@

@h2 Step 3 — Dispatch a node use three ways h2@

@ul
@li @strong The opaque node. strong@ When the name equals @code RESERVED_OPAQUE code@
(the reserved @code node code@), you're at the
@@@code @node(type X) code@@@ extension point — read the @code type code@
parameter and emit your widget. li@
@li @strong Core vocabulary. strong@ When @code isCoreVocabName code@ is true,
take your built-in branch for @code h1 code@, @code table code@, and the rest. li@
@li @strong A user definition. strong@ Otherwise it's a definition that
@code expand code@ has already inlined — pass the body straight through. li@
ul@

@h2 Step 4 — Read parameters h2@

Each node use carries a list of parameters; find one by name and read its value,
as the @code param code@ helper above does.

@h2 Step 5 — Know what @code expand code@ removes h2@

A surviving definition, conditional, iteration, interpolation, or body slot in
the tree you're walking signals a pre-expansion tree or an expansion failure —
error on it or drop it, don't render it.

@strong Result. strong@ The walk above turned an @strong h1 strong@, a paragraph
with italic and bold runs, and a small list into
@code # A Tiny Report code@, @code Some _emphasised_ and **strong** prose. code@,
and two @code - code@ bullets — verified as plain text.

@h2 The AST surface h2@

A block is a paragraph, comment, node use, definition, data definition,
reference, conditional, iteration, or script block. An inline is text, italic,
bold, interpolation, body slot, node use, script call, or comment. A node use
carries its name, parameters, body, and flags. Inspect any file's tree first with
@code wit parse path/to/file.wit code@, which prints the AST as JSON.

@h2 Pitfalls h2@

@ul
@li @strong Sync @code fileReader code@. strong@ As in embedding, @code resolve code@
takes a synchronous @code fileReader code@, not an async @code readFile code@ —
older prose is stale here. li@
@li @strong Handle scripts and comments. strong@ Decide explicitly what to do with
a script block (evaluate it if you ship a script runtime, else drop it) — the
shipped Markdown renderer drops comments and scripts. li@
ul@

@h2 Grounding h2@

The two shipped renderers are the working reference — the HTML renderer and the
Markdown renderer, each with a green test suite. The Markdown renderer's dispatch
— hand-rolled mappings for @code h1 code@ through @code table code@, transparent
sectioning wrappers, and unknown uses passed through unwrapped — is the model to
copy.

